 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know that Torah oaths exempt rather than entitle?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Derived from Exodus 22:10.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.45a.11",
   "daf": "45a",
   "sentenceId": 10,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ כׇּל הַנִּשְׁבָּעִין שֶׁבַּתּוֹרָה נִשְׁבָּעִין וְלֹא מְשַׁלְּמִין. מְנָלַן? דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וְלָקַח בְּעָלָיו וְלֹא יְשַׁלֵּם״ – מִי שֶׁעָלָיו לְשַׁלֵּם, לוֹ שְׁבוּעָה.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The mishna teaches: «All» those «who take an oath that» is legislated «by the Torah take an oath and do not pay.» The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive that oaths mandated by Torah law serve only to exempt one from payment? We derive it from the fact «that the verse states:» “The oath of the Lord shall be between them both, to see whether he has not put his hand on his neighbor’s goods; «and its owner shall accept it, and he shall not make restitution”» (Exodus 22:10). According to the verse, with regard to «he who would» otherwise «need to pay,» it is «on him» that the obligation to take the «oath» is imposed."
  }
 ],
